    {
      "slideNumber": 9,
      "title": "SENTENCE FORMATION",
      "paragraphs": [
        {
          "text": "There are two basic way to form a sentence in a tagalog",
          "isBullet": false
        },
        {
          "text": "I.Subject -predicate word order",
          "isBullet": false
        },
        {
          "text": "II.Inverted or transposed sentence order",
          "isBullet": false
        }
      ],
      "images": []
    },
    {
      "slideNumber": 10,
      "title": "1.SUBJECT-PREDICATE WORD/SENTENCE ORDER",
      "paragraphs": [
        {
          "text": "Where the subject appears at the beginning of the sentence . A sentence in this order when the subject is the most important element in the sentence and it comes first within the sentence. In this order, the word AY (is) &amp; (are) is used.",
          "isBullet": false
        },
        {
          "text": "Subject- tell us who or what the sentence is about Predicate tells us what the subject does or is . It contains the verb and all the words related to action or state.",
          "isBullet": false
        }
      ],
      "images": []
    },
    {
      "slideNumber": 11,
      "title": "Example: Halimbawa",
      "paragraphs": [
        {
          "text": "1. Ang bahay ay Malaki. The house is big.",
          "isBullet": false
        },
        {
          "text": "2. Si Peter ay mabait .\t\t\tPeter is good.",
          "isBullet": false
        },
        {
          "text": "3. Sina Peter at Mary ay mga bata.\t\tPeter and Mary are children.",
          "isBullet": false
        },
        {
          "text": "4. Ang mga bata ay kumakain . The children are eating.",
          "isBullet": false
        },
        {
          "text": "5.Ang babae at lalaki ay nagluluto The woman and the man are cooking.",
          "isBullet": false
        }
      ],
      "images": []
    },
    {
      "slideNumber": 12,
      "title": "THE INVERTED OR TRANSPOSED SENTENCE ORDER",
      "paragraphs": [
        {
          "text": "A sentence is in the inverted or transposed word order when the predicate comes at the beginning of the sentence before the subject. The word AY is not used the inverted word order sentence. Example: Halimbawa",
          "isBullet": false
        },
        {
          "text": "Malaki ang bahay The house is big\t\tAng bahay ay malaki",
          "isBullet": false
        },
        {
          "text": "Kumakain ang bata\t\t\tThe child is eating",
          "isBullet": false
        },
        {
          "text": "Mabait si Peter\t\t\tPeter is good/kind",
          "isBullet": false
        }
      ],
      "images": []
    },
    {
      "slideNumber": 13,
      "title": "Slide 13",
      "paragraphs": [],
      "images": []
    },
    {
      "slideNumber": 14,
      "title": "The focus or emphasis in such sentence is on the predicate rather than on the subject .",
      "paragraphs": [
        {
          "text": "In inverted word order sentences having plural nouns as their subject , the word mga -not ang mga comes before the first nouns .",
          "isBullet": false
        },
        {
          "text": "Example: Halimbawa",
          "isBullet": false
        },
        {
          "text": "Mga lungsod ang Manila at Cebu\t\t\tManila and Cebu are cities",
          "isBullet": false
        },
        {
          "text": "Mga babae sina Lisa at Cha\t\t\t\tLisa and Cha are women",
          "isBullet": false
        }
      ],
      "images": []
